2$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f76db6621ff2252a1cc6e387","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_2_2b_risk_insurance_standard_deviation_premium_principle_694a13ea_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Losses $X,Y$ have equal means and finite second moments, with $\\operatorname{Var}(X)\\le\\operatorname{Var}(Y)$. Both use $\\Pi(Z)=\\mathbb{E}[Z]+\\alpha\\sigma_Z$, where $\\sigma_Z$ is standard deviation and the same $\\alpha>0$ is used. Why is $\\Pi(X)\\le\\Pi(Y)$?","options":["The common mean cancels, and square roots preserve the variance comparison.","Equal means remove the loading, so both premiums equal the common mean.","The larger variance requires a higher coefficient for Y in this definition.","A common positive coefficient reverses the direction of the variance order."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"669ad1d481a37b93d5779797","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_2_2b_risk_insurance_standard_deviation_premium_principle_694a13ea_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A bounded nonconstant loss has mean $\\mu$, positive standard deviation $\\sigma$, and essential maximum b. No rip-off for its SD price requires $\\mu+\\alpha\\sigma\\le b$. Which restriction is equivalent for every such loss law?","options":["$\\alpha\\le(b-\\mu)/\\sigma$.","$\\alpha\\ge(b-\\mu)/\\sigma$.","$\\alpha\\le(b+\\mu)/\\sigma$.","$\\alpha\\le(b-\\mu)/\\sigma^2$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0c3b9bcb9879bdfbc4618d1d","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"}]}}